There are several ways to parse and manipulate XML data in a C++ program using Ruby code. One way is to use the Ruby library Nokogiri, which provides a powerful and efficient interface for working with XML and HTML documents.
To use Nokogiri in a C++ program, you can embed the Ruby interpreter in your C++ code and use the Nokogiri API to parse and manipulate the XML data. Here is an example of how to use Nokogiri to parse the XML data representing a binary search tree:
```cpp
#include <ruby.h>
#include <nokogiri.h>
// Parse the XML data and return a Nokogiri document object
VALUE parse_xml(const char* xml_data) {
  VALUE nokogiri = rb_const_get(rb_cObject, rb_intern("Nokogiri"));
  VALUE parse_options = rb_const_get(nokogiri, rb_intern("XML_PARSE_NOBLANKS"));
  VALUE parse_method = rb_const_get(nokogiri, rb_intern("XML"));
  return rb_funcall(nokogiri, rb_intern("XML"), 2, rb_str_new_cstr(xml_data), parse_options);
}
// Traverse the binary search tree and print its values in order
void traverse_tree(VALUE node) {
  if (node == Qnil) {
    return;
  }
  traverse_tree(rb_funcall(node, rb_intern("left"), 0));
  printf("%d ", NUM2INT(rb_funcall(rb_funcall(node, rb_intern("value"), 0), rb_intern("text"), 0)));
  traverse_tree(rb_funcall(node, rb_intern("right"), 0));
}
int main() {
  // XML data representing a binary search tree
  const char* xml_data = "<root><node><value>8</value><left><node><value>3</value><left><node><value>1</value></node><node><value>6</value></node></left><right><node><value>7</value></node></right></node></left><right><node><value>10</value><right><node><value>14</value><left><node><value>13</value></node></left></node></right></node></right></node></root>";
  // Initialize the Ruby interpreter
  ruby_init();
  ruby_init_loadpath();
  // Parse the XML data
  VALUE doc = parse_xml(xml_data);
  // Traverse the binary search tree and print its values in order
  traverse_tree(rb_funcall(rb_funcall(doc, rb_intern("root"), 0), rb_intern("node"), 0));
  // Clean up the Ruby interpreter
  ruby_cleanup(0);
  return 0;
}
```
In this example, the `parse_xml` function uses the Nokogiri API to parse the XML data and return a Nokogiri document object. The `traverse_tree` function recursively traverses the binary search tree and prints its values in order. Finally, the `main` function initializes the Ruby interpreter, parses the XML data, traverses the binary search tree, and cleans up the Ruby interpreter.
Using Ruby code to parse and manipulate XML data in a C++ program can improve efficiency and streamline code implementation by leveraging the powerful and flexible features of the Ruby language and its libraries.

