Here's a Java algorithm that can efficiently calculate the prime numbers ranging from 1 to 1000, filter out those with non-perfect square roots, and then compute their summation for output:
```
public class PrimeSum {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int limit = 1000;
        int sum = 0;
        boolean[] primes = new boolean[limit + 1];
        for (int i = 2; i <= limit; i++) {
            primes[i] = true;
        }
        for (int i = 2; i <= Math.sqrt(limit); i++) {
            if (primes[i]) {
                for (int j = i * i; j <= limit; j += i) {
                    primes[j] = false;
                }
            }
        }
        for (int i = 2; i <= limit; i++) {
            if (primes[i] && Math.sqrt(i) % 1 == 0) {
                sum += i;
            }
        }
        System.out.println("The sum of prime numbers with perfect square roots from 1 to 1000 is " + sum);
    }
}
```
Here's how the algorithm works:
1. We set the limit to 1000, initialize the sum to 0, and create a boolean array called `primes` with a size of `limit + 1`. The `primes` array will be used to keep track of whether a number is prime or not.
2. We initialize all elements of the `primes` array to `true`, except for the first two elements (0 and 1), which we know are not prime.
3. We loop through all numbers from 2 to the square root of the limit. For each number, if it is marked as prime in the `primes` array, we mark all of its multiples as not prime. We do this by starting at the square of the current number (since all smaller multiples will have already been marked as not prime) and incrementing by the current number.
4. We loop through all numbers from 2 to the limit. For each number, if it is marked as prime in the `primes` array and its square root is a perfect square (i.e., its square root has no decimal component), we add it to the `sum`.
5. We output the `sum`.
This algorithm uses the Sieve of Eratosthenes to efficiently find all prime numbers up to the limit. It then checks each prime number to see if its square root is a perfect square, and adds it to the `sum` if it is. The algorithm has a time complexity of O(n log log n) and a space complexity of O(n).

