Here's an example implementation of the Sieve of Eratosthenes algorithm in Java:
```java
public static List<Integer> generatePrimes(int n) {
    boolean[] isPrime = new boolean[n + 1];
    Arrays.fill(isPrime, true);
    isPrime[0] = false;
    isPrime[1] = false;
    for (int i = 2; i <= Math.sqrt(n); i++) {
        if (isPrime[i]) {
            for (int j = i * i; j <= n; j += i) {
                isPrime[j] = false;
            }
        }
    }
    List<Integer> primes = new ArrayList<>();
    for (int i = 2; i <= n; i++) {
        if (isPrime[i]) {
            primes.add(i);
        }
    }
    return primes;
}
```
To optimize the program for large limit values, we can use a segmented version of the Sieve of Eratosthenes algorithm. Here's an example implementation:
```java
public static List<Integer> generatePrimes(int n) {
    int segmentSize = (int) Math.sqrt(n);
    List<Integer> primes = new ArrayList<>();
    boolean[] isPrime = new boolean[segmentSize];
    Arrays.fill(isPrime, true);
    for (int i = 2; i <= Math.sqrt(n); i++) {
        if (isPrime[i % segmentSize]) {
            for (int j = i * i; j <= n; j += i) {
                if (j < segmentSize) {
                    isPrime[j] = false;
                } else if (j % segmentSize == i % segmentSize) {
                    isPrime[j / segmentSize] = false;
                }
            }
        }
    }
    for (int i = 2; i < segmentSize; i++) {
        if (isPrime[i]) {
            primes.add(i);
        }
    }
    int numSegments = (n - 1) / segmentSize;
    for (int i = 1; i <= numSegments; i++) {
        Arrays.fill(isPrime, true);
        int start = i * segmentSize;
        for (int j = 2; j <= Math.sqrt(n); j++) {
            if (isPrime[j % segmentSize]) {
                int k = Math.max(j * j, (start + j - 1) / j * j);
                for (; k < start + segmentSize; k += j) {
                    isPrime[k - start] = false;
                }
            }
        }
        for (int j = 0; j < segmentSize && start + j <= n; j++) {
            if (isPrime[j]) {
                primes.add(start + j);
            }
        }
    }
    return primes;
}
```
This implementation divides the range of numbers into segments of size sqrt(n) and generates the prime numbers for each segment separately. This reduces the amount of memory required by the program and also improves performance for large values of n.

